<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">P. V. Narasimha Rao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a>–<a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Pamulaparthi Venkata Narasimha Rao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">P. V. Narasimha Rao was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> politician who served as prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1991 to 1996. Taking office during a severe balance-of-payments crisis, he and his finance minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manmohan-singh">Manmohan Singh</a> dismantled the licence system, devalued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupee</a> and opened the economy to foreign trade and investment. His government also faced the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babri-masjid">Babri Masjid</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> in 1992 and the communal violence that followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/p-v-narasimha-rao.jpg" alt="P. V. Narasimha Rao" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Lambiotte - European Commission · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visit_of_Narasimha_Rao,_Indian_Minister_for_Foreign_Affairs,_to_the_CEC_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pamulaparthi Venkata Narasimha Rao was born on 28 June 1921 into a Telugu Niyogi Brahmin family in Laknepalli village in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, then part of Hyderabad State. At the age of three he was adopted by Pamulaparthi Ranga Rao and Rukminamma and taken to the village of Vangara. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osmania-university">Osmania University</a>, took a master's degree in law at Hislop College and completed his law studies at Fergusson College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>. In 1931, aged ten, he was married to Satyamma, who died in 1970.</p><p>He was an active participant in the independence movement and joined full-time politics after independence as a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>. He sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> assembly from 1957 to 1977 and held ministerial posts from 1962 to 1973. As chief minister of Andhra Pradesh from 1971 he applied land reforms and land ceiling acts strictly; President's rule had to be imposed to counter the Jai Andhra movement. In the union government he later held the Defence, Home Affairs and External Affairs portfolios.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In the 1991 general election the Congress under Rao won 244 seats, and with outside support from other parties he formed a minority government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was then close to bankruptcy. I. G. Patel declined the post of finance minister, and Rao chose the economist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manmohan-singh">Manmohan Singh</a>, a former governor of the Reserve Bank of India. The reforms advanced furthest in opening to foreign investment, reforming capital markets, deregulating domestic business and changing the trade regime. In 1992 the Controller of Capital Issues was abolished. Some socialist nationalists criticised the liberalisation at the time.</p><p>On 6 December 1992 members of the Vishva Hindu Parishad demolished the Babri Mosque at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>. The act led to large-scale communal violence, the most extensive since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">Partition of India</a>, in almost every major city. His government introduced the Terrorist and Disruptive Activities (Prevention) Act, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s first anti-terrorism legislation. In 1993 an earthquake at Latur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> killed nearly 10,000 people, and Rao's relief operation drew applause. Voters turned the Congress out in the 1996 election, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atal-bihari-vajpayee">Atal Bihari Vajpayee</a> became prime minister on 16 May 1996.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rao published a novel, The Insider, whose rise of a man through Indian politics resembles events in his own life. He spoke 17 languages, and translated a Telugu work by Viswanatha Satyanarayana into Hindi and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> novel into Telugu. He suffered a heart attack on 9 December 2004 and died 14 days later, aged 83, and seven days of state mourning were declared. Biographies by Vinay Sitapati and studies by Sanjay Baru and Jairam Ramesh examine his role in the reforms of 1991. He was awarded the Bharat Ratna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s highest civilian award, posthumously in 2024.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “P. V. Narasimha Rao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government of P.V. <b>Narasimha Rao</b>, with Manmohan Singh as finance minister, responded by dismantling the licence system, opening the economy to foreign investment and reducing tariffs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Laknepalli, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.95,79.83&amp;year=1974" aria-label="Laknepalli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of P. V. Narasimha Rao"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4177 5986h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4177 5986h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.95,79.83&amp;year=1974">What was here in 1974? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
